        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "s5qxxbv",
          "x": 84,
          "y": 4104,
          "name": "Notes",
          "fill": "$ink",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 4140,
          "content": "• Stage frame (dark carved wood, cordel border): post (tiles vertically) and bar (tiles horizontally) share one 90 px cross-section: rule / cut saw-teeth / rule / lozenge-and-grain field / rule / ticks / rule. Their butt ends have no border so repeats join seamlessly.\n• Frame anchors: theatre_frame_corner is open on its right and bottom edges; the bar butts into its right edge at y 30..120 and the post into its bottom edge at x 30..120. theatre_frame_foot is open at the top: the post butts into it at x 135..225, y 0; the foot's base sits on the ground line (y 147, 92%). For the right-hand side, mirror the corner and the foot.\n• theatre_paper: handmade-paper fibres, bark specks and faint creases as solid Ink, about 9-10% coverage, tiles both ways (seams checked).\n• theatre_valance: carved top band, five scalloped swags between gathered knots at x = 90 + 284k (k 0..5), a carved tassel under each knot, a cascade at each end.\n• theatre_curtain_1.2 are drawn as the LEFT curtain (outer edge on the left, stage edge on the right); mirror them for the right side. 1 hangs straight with an eyelet heading; 2 is gathered and tied back at y ~560 with a cord and tassel. Deep folds are cut-out slits that notch the hem.\n• theatre_rod_1.3: a stick with a pin-hole tab at the top (it pins onto a puppet joint) and a handle at the bottom. 1 straight with a bulb handle, 2 a crooked twig with a cord-wrapped handle, 3 tapered with a hook and a cross-hatched grip. The 40 px frame makes standard line weights under 1.5 px, so cuts are 1.8-2.2 px.\n• theatre_audience_1.6 (hat, braid, bow, bald head, headscarf, child on shoulders) are backs of heads with the shoulders running off the bottom edge, so they line up as a front row along the bottom of the screen. They are not ground-line props.\n• Boitatá puppet: the body band is 76 px thick in every piece. Each segment is 240 px long (x 8..248) with pin holes 30 px in from each end (x 38 and 218), so neighbours pinned hole-on-hole overlap by 60 px, a quarter. The head's pin is at its neck end (lower left) and the tail's at its left end. Fire-tongue cut-outs run along the back of the neck, every segment and the tail; the head has two round eye holes cut through, facing right like serpent_head.\n• Caçador puppet: the prop_cacador figure redrawn in profile facing right (meia-lua hat with the star, moustache, vest, cross strap, satchel, rifle across the shoulder). The body has pin holes at the shoulder and hip; the arm and both legs (1 straight, 2 bent at the knee) have the matching pin at their top.\n• puppet_tree_1.3 (araucária, buriti, sumaúma) are simplified silhouettes with large cut-outs, centred, base on the ground line (y 828), with a rod pin hole in the trunk foot.\n• puppet_moon: a disc with carved craters and a short rod stub pinned at the bottom. puppet_waves: four curling crests with a period of 256 px, tiles horizontally.\n• puppet_onca: the prop_onca art reused through penref (rosettes, snarl), with the tail cut off at the rump on a round boss. Pin hole at the tail root; puppet_onca_tail has the matching pin on its base boss.\n• Pin-hole radius is 5-6 px throughout. In the small puppet frames, cut widths are HEAVY-based (~2.6 px), because FINE would be under 1.5 px.\n• Nothing missing: all 35 S5 assets are present at their exact sizes.",
          "lineHeight": 1.5,
          "fontFamily": "Inter",
          "fontSize": 20,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        }
      ]
    },
